			"id": "103",
			"headline": "New Search Engine 'NotGoogle' Only Shows Results That Make You Feel Bad About Your Choices",
			"summary": "The 'RegretSearch' algorithm prioritizes results that question your decisions, career path, and relationships.",
			"fullBody": "The self-improvement industry has been revolutionized by 'NotGoogle,' a search engine specifically designed to make you feel worse about your life choices. Unlike traditional search engines that help you find information, RegretSearch prioritizes results that question everything. When you search 'Is my job good?' the results show 'The average person changes careers 47 times, and yours isn't on the list.' When you search 'Should I have gone to college?' it shows 'People who went to college make 47% more, but people who didn't have more free time to regret their decisions.' One user said, 'I searched --Am I a good parent?-- and it showed me a list of things I've done wrong, ranked by severity.' The search engine has been credited with 'making people more aware of their mistakes' and 'terrible for mental health.'",
